    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0247",
      "sequence": 259,
      "meaning": "Sītā’s splendour cannot be described: the Mother of the world is a treasury of form and virtue. Every comparison seems too slight and is tainted by attachment to some ordinary woman; what bad poet would earn disgrace by comparing Sītā to her? If she is measured against another woman, where in the world is any maiden so lovely? Sarasvatī is talkative, Bhavānī has only half a body, and Rati grieves because her husband has no body. How could one call Vaidehī equal to Lakṣmī, whose dear brother is poison and whose other brother is wine? Suppose beauty’s nectar were an ocean, its tortoise the embodiment of perfect form, its churning rope loveliness, its Mount Mandara adornment, and Love himself churned it with his own lotus hands: even if Lakṣmī, root of beauty and joy, arose in this way, poets would compare her to Sītā only with hesitation.",
