    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0249",
      "sequence": 261,
      "meaning": "On seeing Rāma’s form and Sītā’s beauty, men and women abandon blinking. Everyone worries but is shy to speak, so inwardly they petition the Creator: quickly remove Janaka’s obstinacy and give him an understanding pleasing to us; let the king abandon his unconsidered vow and arrange the marriage of Sītā and Rāma. The whole world would call it good and every heart desires it, whereas persistence in the vow will bring only inward burning. All are submerged in this longing: the dark-hued bridegroom alone is worthy of Jānakī. Janaka then summons the heralds; reciting formal praises, they approach, and at his command proceed joyfully to proclaim mighty Videha’s vow to every ruler.",
